  ** OpenCvSharp4.runtime.osx.arm64 and OpenCvSharp4.runtime.win: the wrapper
     package itself declares Apache-2.0 (verified via .nuspec, both
     4.13.0.20260627). Both bundle statically-linked native third-party
     code beyond OpenCV itself, confirmed by extracting build-configuration
     strings and copyright strings from the shipped binaries themselves
     (`strings runtimes/*/native/*`), since neither nupkg bundles a
     LICENSE/COPYING/NOTICE file for any of this — only README.runtime.md:

       - FFmpeg. Statically linked into libOpenCvSharpExtern.dylib on
         osx-arm64 (per README.runtime.md: "macOS ... packages include
         FFmpeg ... statically linked"); shipped as a separate, dynamically
         loaded opencv_videoio_ffmpeg4130_64.dll on win-x64. Embedded
         configure strings show NEITHER --enable-gpl NOR --enable-nonfree
         on either platform, and the osx-arm64 build explicitly
         --disable-libx264 --disable-libx265 (the two codecs that force
         FFmpeg to GPL); win-x64 only enables --enable-libopenh264
         --enable-libvpx --enable-libaom, all permissively (BSD-style)
         licensed. That is consistent with this same package family's own
         README.runtime.md statement for its Linux variant — "the full
         (non-slim) package includes FFmpeg (LGPL v2.1) ... statically
         linked" — and is the basis for calling FFmpeg here LGPL-2.1+
         rather than GPL. UNDETERMINED WITH CERTAINTY: no FFmpeg
         LICENSE.md/COPYING text is bundled in either nupkg, so this rests
         on binary-string inspection plus the vendor's parallel statement,
         not on a first-party licence file — flagged rather than smoothed
         over, per PLAN risk 7. Static linking under LGPL-2.1 also carries
         a relinking obligation (§6) that this notice does not yet resolve.
       - libjpeg-turbo (IJG-style/BSD, "Copyright (C) 1991-2025 The
         libjpeg-turbo Project and many others") and libpng (the
         libpng/zlib licence, "Copyright (c) 1998-2002,2004,2006-2018 Glenn
         Randers-Pehrson" et al.) are also statically linked, per copyright
         strings found the same way. Both are permissive and do not affect
         GPLv3 compatibility.

     Chase the LGPL-2.1 relinking obligation and a citable FFmpeg licence
     text before a wide release; nothing above blocks GPLv3 as currently
     built.

AvaloniaUI.DiagnosticsSupport (2.2.3) declares NO licence at all — its
.nuspec has no <license>/<licenseUrl> element, and the package contains no
LICENSE/COPYING file (only README.md, an icon, and build props/targets),
confirmed by inspecting both directly in the NuGet cache. This is not an
oversight in this notices file; the package itself ships unlicensed. It is
referenced unconditionally by src/LoreFetch.App/App.csproj and would
therefore ship in the single-file publish as built today. A separate,
already-approved plan makes the reference Debug-only; until that lands,
LoreFetch is redistributing a package with no stated redistribution rights
whenever it publishes a Release build.


